  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 544 AM EDT TUE SEP 22 2026\n\nThe long-term period opens Wednesday evening with a closed ~581\ndam low near Cincinnati, OH, and a closed ~587 dam upper-level\nhigh north of Lake Superior. These will be mirrored at the \nsurface by a broad high pressure area over eastern Canada and the\nNortheast, while lee-side troughing lingers over the western \nfoothills of the central and southern Appalachians.\n\nAt the start of the period, convection will likely be ongoing in\nthe vicinity of that lee-side trough over portions of eastern\nKentucky, but should wane Wednesday night and Thursday as the\nupper-level low drifts southeast toward the Carolinas. By Friday,\nthe upper-level low will be supporting a strong autumn coastal \nlow riding up the East Coast, while the aforementioned surface \nhigh will have expanded southwestward over the Great Lakes, \nplacing the Ohio Valley under a drying northeasterly flow. The \npattern remains fairly benign for us heading through the weekend--\nthe coastal low becomes vertically stacked off the Mid-Atlantic \nand New England coast while the upper-level ridge to our north \nweakens and merges with ridging over the central CONUS. The \nattendant Canadian surface high weakens, becomes more amorphous, \nand modifies at more southerly latitudes.\n\nSensible weather impacts will feature waning shower and\nthunderstorm chances Wednesday night, leaving just a few stray\nshowers for Thursday. Clouds decrease with time, yielding\nmostly clear to partly cloudy skies Thursday night through\nMonday, with an exception being routine late night/morning fog \nthrough favored sheltered river valleys. The most notable sensible\nchange will be temperatures though: Highs will range through the \n70s in most places on Thursday, and in the upper 60s to mid 70s \non Friday and Saturday. Nighttime lows will turn cool, even a bit\nchilly, starting off in the 50s on Wednesday night and settling \ndeep into the 40s by Friday and Saturday nights -- perhaps \nnearing 40F in the coldest hollows. A warming trend does ensue by\nearly next week with daytime highs recovering into the mid 70s to\nlow 80s by Monday and lows moderating back closer to 50F.",
